<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TestPlatformRoot Condition="$(TestPlatformRoot) == ''">..\..\..\</TestPlatformRoot>
    <TestProject>true</TestProject>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Import Project="$(TestPlatformRoot)scripts/build/TestPlatform.Settings.targets" />
  <PropertyGroup Label="Configuration">
    <RootNamespace>Microsoft.TestPlatform.Extensions.EventLogCollector.UnitTests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- This project used to set BOTH <TargetFramework>net451</TargetFramework> here and
         <TargetFrameworks>net451</TargetFrameworks> below, and it did so from the day it was
         created: 468d4b232, 2017-09-07, "Added Event Log Data Collector for Windows OS", which
         added this file with both properties already present. Four commits in the file's entire
         history and the defect was in the first.

         It was the only one of 72 test projects in this repository that set both, and it was the
         only one whose tests did not run: its .trx carried zero results and a RunInfo warning
         saying no test was available because no discoverer was registered. The MSTest adapter was
         missing from its output directory while present for the other 13 net451 test projects, and
         NuGet delivers that adapter through build assets which are imported per inner build.
         Setting both properties makes the inner/outer distinction ambiguous.

         So these 36 tests had NEVER run, on any build, since 2017 - not "stopped running", never.
         15.9 traced the identical commit on their line independently.

         HOW TO VERIFY THE SINGULAR PROPERTY IS ACTUALLY GONE, because this comment defeats the
         obvious check. Searching this file for the literal "<TargetFramework>" returns hits - in
         THIS COMMENT - while zero such elements exist:

           grep for the literal                             hits, all of them prose, all in here
           [xml] parse, SelectNodes('//TargetFramework')     0 elements
           [xml] parse, SelectNodes('//TargetFrameworks')    1 element

         Parse the XML, or anchor to a line that is not a comment. A text search cannot distinguish
         a defect from the note explaining that it was removed - and here the text that defeats the
         search is the documentation of the fix itself.

         No hit COUNT is stated above, deliberately. The first draft of this comment said "1 hit",
         and adding these two paragraphs made it 3 - the comment falsified its own number before it
         was ever committed. A count of occurrences in the same text that contains the count cannot
         stay true. The element counts are safe to state because they are properties of the XML,
         which prose cannot change. -->
    <TargetFrameworks>net451</TargetFrameworks>
    <AssemblyName>Microsoft.TestPlatform.Extensions.EventLogCollector.UnitTests</AssemblyName>
    <WarningsAsErrors>true</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\src\DataCollectors\Microsoft.TestPlatform.Extensions.EventLogCollector\Microsoft.TestPlatform.Extensions.EventLogCollector.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Reference Include="System.Runtime" />
    <Reference Include="System" />
    <Reference Include="Microsoft.CSharp" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <Import Project="$(TestPlatformRoot)scripts\build\TestPlatform.targets" />
</Project>
